Object.assign(WEAPON, {
  torpedo(t) { noiseHit(t, {dur:.2, bp:300, gain:.5}); whoosh(t + .1, 2.2, 200, 500, .25); explosion(t + 2.4, 1.6); noiseHit(t + 2.5, {dur:2.5, lp:300, gain:.5, attack:.3}); },   // the hiss of the run, then the column of water
  torpedoRun(t) { engine(t, 2, {f:80, gain:.12, lp:800, ramp:1.3}); whoosh(t + 1, 1.4, 250, 600, .2); explosion(t + 2.5, 1.5); },
  depth(t) { for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) { noiseHit(t + i * .25, {dur:.08, bp:500, gain:.3}); thump(t + 1.4 + i * .35, {f0:50, f1:20, dur:1.2, gain:1}); noiseHit(t + 1.5 + i * .35, {dur:1.5, lp:200, gain:.5, attack:.1}); } tone(t, {f:1600, dur:.5, gain:.06, type:'sine'}); },   // asdic ping, then the charges going off deep
  bigguns(t) { for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) { cannon(t + i * .2, 2.4, .8); } whistle(t + 1.2, 1.3, 1200, 400); for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) explosion(t + 2.6 + i * .15, 1.6); },
  charge(t) { for (let i = 0; i < 14; i++) thump(t + i * .09, {f0:120, f1:70, dur:.08, gain:.35}); UI_SND.shout(t + .2); UI_SND.shout(t + .7); for (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) report(t + 1 + i * .3, .9, 1); },   // hooves, a cheer, then the carbines
});

/* ---------- unit notes and specifications ---------- */
Object.assign(UNIT_INFO, {
  'pl:cav':'The legend of Polish lancers charging tanks is German propaganda. At Krojanty on 1 September 1939 uhlans did charge German infantry with sabres, and were caught by armoured cars. Polish cavalry fought as mounted infantry, with anti-tank rifles and 37 mm guns, and fought well.',
  'pl:tank':'The 7TP was a Polish development of the Vickers 6-ton with a diesel engine and a 37 mm Bofors gun that could knock out any German tank of 1939. Only about 140 were built.',
  'pl:at':'The Wz. 35 anti-tank rifle was so secret that its crates were marked "Ur" (for export to Uruguay) and troops were only trained on it days before the war. It could pierce every German tank of 1939 at 300 m.',
  'fr40:htank':'The Char B1 bis had 60 mm of armour and two guns. At Stonne on 16 May 1940 Captain Billotte\'s B1 "Eure" knocked out 13 German tanks and took 140 hits without being penetrated. But it needed refuelling every few hours, and a crew of four was too few.',
  'fr40:mtank':'The SOMUA S35 was probably the best tank of 1940: cast armour, a 47 mm gun and good speed. Its weakness was the one-man turret, where the commander also had to load and aim.',
  'ger40:tank':'The Panzer II was a training tank pressed into battle with a 20 mm gun. In 1940 most German tanks were Panzer Is and IIs. They won through radios, tactics and air support, not armour.',
  'ger40:pak':'The 8.8 cm Flak 18 was an anti-aircraft gun. At Arras on 21 May 1940 Rommel personally directed 88s and field howitzers firing over open sights to stop British Matildas that his 37 mm guns could not penetrate.',
  'uk40:mtank':'The Matilda II had 78 mm of armour, immune to every German anti-tank gun in France. At Arras its shells bounced off... and German 88s did not. In the desert in 1940-41, when Italian guns could not get through its armour, it was called the "Queen of the Desert".',
  'uk40:esc':'The Flower-class corvette was built in merchant yards on a whale-catcher design. It would "roll on wet grass", but 267 were built and they kept the Atlantic convoys alive in 1940-42.',
  'uk40:tb':'The Fairey Swordfish "Stringbag" was a 1934 biplane so slow that Bismarck\'s gunners, aiming ahead of it, missed. One torpedo jammed her rudder on 26 May 1941 and sealed her fate.',
  'jp:ftr':'The A6M2 Zero outclassed every Allied fighter in the Pacific in 1941-42 with its range and agility. It had no armour and no self-sealing tanks, and lost its veteran pilots at Midway and Guadalcanal.',
  'jp:tank':'The Type 95 Ha-Go was a light tank with a 37 mm gun. In Malaya it was enough: the British had almost no tanks, and the Ha-Gos charged down the trunk roads through the rubber plantations.',
  'jp:dd':'The Type 93 "Long Lance" torpedo ran on pure oxygen: 20 km at 48 knots, almost no wake, and a 490 kg warhead. The Allies did not know it existed until 1943.',
  'jp:mortar':'The Type 89 grenade discharger was nicknamed the "knee mortar" by the Americans. Marines who tried firing it braced on the thigh broke their femurs.',
  'cn:eng':'At Xifengkou in March 1933 the 29th Army\'s dadao (broadsword) teams made night raids on Japanese positions. The "Song of the Big Sword" became a wartime anthem.',
  'cn:ftr':'The American Volunteer Group, the "Flying Tigers", flew P-40Bs for China from December 1941, painted with shark mouths copied from an RAF squadron in North Africa.',
  'usp:apc':'The LVT amtrac could swim and crawl over coral. At Tarawa there were only 125, enough for the first three waves. Everyone after them waded ashore from the reef.',
  'usp:htank':'The Zippo flame tank threw napalm 100 m into caves and bunkers. On Iwo Jima and Okinawa it was the weapon the Japanese defenders feared most.',
  'ger:sub':'The Type VIIC was the workhorse U-boat: 568 built, crew of 44, 14 torpedoes. Of 40,000 U-boat men, some 30,000 died, the highest loss rate of any service in the war.',
  'ger40:sub':'The Type VIIC was the workhorse U-boat: 568 built, crew of 44, 14 torpedoes. Of 40,000 U-boat men, some 30,000 died, the highest loss rate of any service in the war.',
  'usp:bb':'USS Washington\'s radar-directed 16-inch guns sank Kirishima at 8,400 yards at night on 15 November 1942: the only time in the war one battleship sank another one-on-one.',
  'jp:bb':'Yamato, the largest battleship ever built, carried nine 18.1-inch guns. Her only surface action was off Samar against escort carriers and destroyers. Sunk by aircraft on 7 April 1945.',
  'su41:htank':'The KV-1 shocked the Germans in 1941: at Raseiniai a single KV-2 blocked the 6th Panzer Division\'s supply road for a day, shrugging off 37 mm and 50 mm hits.',
  'su41:cav':'Soviet cavalry corps raided deep behind German lines in the winter of 1941-42, where tanks froze and horses still moved. General Belov\'s 1st Guards Cavalry Corps spent five months behind German lines.',
});
